# Docs and Storybook

- Public examples must use idiomatic Vue and compile.
- Each public component needs basic usage plus its important variants and states.
- The canonical `DOCTUI_REGISTRY.components` list is the release contract for public visual components. Every registry component must have VitePress documentation, at least one real live VitePress preview, and Storybook coverage imported from its public `@doctui/*` package.
- Run `bun run test:docs-coverage` when public component metadata, package exports, VitePress previews or Storybook coverage changes. Adding a registry component without both documentation surfaces is a regression.
- A text mention is not Storybook coverage. A story must import the component from its public package and actually use it in a story module.
- Low-level code under `packages/core/src/primitives` is internal by default. Do not expose it from the public core entry unless it is deliberately promoted into the canonical registry and receives the same docs, Storybook, metadata and compatibility treatment as other public APIs.
- Storybook should demonstrate behavior, not just static appearance.
- Every public component story module must register the actual component in Storybook metadata and expose functional `args`/`argTypes` Controls for the meaningful public props. The primary `Basic`/`Default`/`Playground` story must consume `args`; a primary story showing `This story has no controls` is a documentation failure.
- Controlled stories that expose `modelValue`/`v-model` through Controls must keep canvas interactions and the Controls panel synchronized. Use Storybook `useArgs` (or the current supported equivalent) to write `update:modelValue` changes back into args; do not use an isolated local `ref` as the primary Playground source of truth.
- Do not add decorative Controls that the story render ignores. Matrix/composition stories may override individual props, but the primary interactive story must respond to Controls.
- Composition stories that intentionally manage multiple independent component states should disable Controls explicitly instead of presenting a misleading Controls panel.
- Document accessibility/keyboard behavior for custom interactive widgets.
- Keep docs metadata structured enough to generate API pages and `llms.txt` later.
- Do not copy Mantine prose verbatim; describe doctui's actual Vue API and behavior.
- Public component work normally requires both VitePress and Storybook. Neither surface replaces the other.
- Storybook should include a realistic advanced composition once related doctui components exist; do not call a single isolated component with many props an advanced example.
- Components with `size` should have a visual size comparison when geometry is meaningful. Input-like components should show important normal/focus/error/disabled/readonly/checked states as applicable.
- When `classNames`/`styles` or public CSS variables are exposed, document at least one supported customization example instead of recommending deep selectors.
- Field docs must state the outer-root versus native-control attribute contract and show accessibility relationships such as labels, description/error and `aria-describedby` composition.
- Keep component metadata synchronized with every documented public prop, style part and non-obvious accessibility behavior.
